statement:
Let n, l >= 0, mu a measure on R^(n+l), q : R^(n+l) -> R, y in R^(n+l), phi : R -> R, and reals S, epsilon, B, t with epsilon > 0. Write r = r(x) = |x - y| and phi' = deriv phi. Assume:
- mu(B_S(y)) < infinity, where B_S(y) is the closed ball;
- q is mu-a.e. strongly measurable and |q(x)| <= B for mu-a.e. x;
- phi is C^1 and nonincreasing, with phi(s) = 1 for s <= 1 and phi(s) = 0 for s >= 1 + epsilon;
- 0 < t and (1 + epsilon) t < S.
Then x -> phi(r/t) q(x) and x -> (r/t) phi'(r/t) q(x) are mu-integrable. Moreover the function s -> integral phi(r/s) q dmu (a Bochner integral, defined for every real s) is differentiable at s = t with derivative -(1/t) integral (r/t) phi'(r/t) q dmu.
